# Line endings are a correctness issue here, not a style preference:
# `.githooks/pre-commit` is a bash script, and a CRLF shebang line fails on Linux/macOS.
# The skills declare `platforms: [linux, macos, windows]`, so the repo must deliver LF.
* text=auto
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.md      text=auto
*.ps1     text eol=crlf

# `*.md` stays `text=auto` because skill Markdown must remain byte-identical to the live tree.
# The two front pages have no live counterpart, so pin them: a CRLF rewrite silently breaks
# multi-line edits to them (it broke three patches during the README work).
README.md     text eol=lf
README.en.md  text eol=lf

# The hook and the shipped engines are expected to be runnable directly.
.githooks/pre-commit   text eol=lf

# The dfs-bootstrap templates land verbatim in somebody else's repo and have no live-tree
# counterpart, so they fall under the same rule as the two front pages above: pin LF. Left to
# `text=auto`, a Windows clone received CRLF for the same shipped asset a Linux clone got as LF
# (measured: 31/16/105/7 CRLF lines across the four files). Gates would not have noticed —
# bootstrap.py reads with universal newlines — which is exactly why it is pinned rather than
# trusted to stay harmless.
dfs-bootstrap/templates/workmemory/*   text eol=lf
